- Attempt 1 and 2 both *forced* partitions onto alternate paths and increased cost, proving the evaluator's model is additive per edge per partition; the min-cost-flow optimum then reuses cheap shared edges. This candidate keeps the strategy's mechanism (independent per-partition flow solve with capacity-aware residual graphs) but makes the per-partition solve cost-minimizing: it picks the cheaper of the base and residual route per (dst, partition), so cost is bounded by the parent's while the disjoint-flow freedom is retained when capacity actually binds.
- Structural change to escape the already-explored 660.70 plateau: closure shortest paths are now computed on the full graph (selfloops only removed), letting relay legs traverse src — cheaper relays the parent structurally forbade. This should strictly lower the arborescence weight, reducing total cost for every configuration.
- Compute remains bounded: one arborescence build plus at most num_partitions−1 residual Dijkstra rounds; incumbent fallback preserved.
